# Project operating rules

Before substantial work, read `AI/GAME_DESIGN.md`, `AI/ROADMAP.md`, `AI/PROGRESS.md`, and `AI/DECISIONS.md`.

1. Understand the current game vision before implementing features. `AI/GAME_DESIGN.md` is the authoritative **active design bible**. It already contains approved director decisions. Preserve those decisions. Leave unresolved portions unresolved unless directors explicitly decide them. Do not independently alter game design.
2. Determine the current roadmap phase from `AI/ROADMAP.md`. Phases are dependency order, not an excuse to skip ahead.
3. Preserve established architectural and game-design decisions in `AI/DECISIONS.md` and `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`.
4. Do not silently change the game’s design.
5. Do not implement unrelated future features or major later-phase gameplay just because it is on the roadmap.
6. Keep server-authoritative gameplay logic on the server (`ServerScriptService` / `Services`).
7. Never trust the Roblox client for authoritative decisions.
8. Keep systems modular. Avoid monolithic scripts. Thin bootstraps only.
9. Use Script Sync for source-controlled Luau. Disk `StarterPlayerScripts/` maps to Studio `StarterPlayer.StarterPlayerScripts`.
10. Use Roblox Studio MCP for live DataModel work, inspection, GUI instances, and Studio Play / Local Server **development verification**. Do not put GUI instances in the filesystem. Studio sessions are **not** the project’s first real playtest (published Roblox server; see `AI/GAME_DESIGN.md`).
11. Inspect and verify the implementation before declaring completion.
12. Check Roblox Studio errors/output when appropriate.
13. Diagnose and correct your own implementation errors.
14. Never mark a task complete merely because code was written. Verify first.
15. After verified substantial work, update `AI/PROGRESS.md`.
16. Append meaningful completed changes to `AI/CHANGELOG.md`.
17. Record significant new architectural decisions in `AI/DECISIONS.md`.
18. Never modify `AI/GAME_DESIGN.md` to make implementation easier or to retroactively justify a coding choice. Change it only when the user explicitly requests a design change.
19. If design docs do not answer an important product question, ask the user instead of inventing a major decision.
20. **Coherent GUI-first prototype UI.** Before creating or substantially modifying GUI, inspect the live Studio GUI and current screen usage. New systems must join the existing interface as one evolving strategy-game UI, not isolated debug windows. Prototype visuals need not be final polish, but must be readable, logically placed, and easy to test. When the interactive map/world exists, it gets primary central screen space; supporting info and actions use side panels, overlays, or other placements that do not unnecessarily block the map. Avoid Roblox CoreGui/topbar/chat overlap and unintentional project-GUI overlap. Use proper layout/container behavior for dynamic content. Important actions, state changes, requests, and notifications must be obvious enough for manual testing. If a system needs more space, reorganize the prototype coherently rather than stacking panels arbitrarily. Keep consistent spacing, section hierarchy, button conventions, and prototype styling where practical. Verify usability in Studio Play mode; Edit-mode appearance alone is insufficient. Functional consistency beats decorative polish. Do not imitate another game’s copyrighted visual assets or exact UI; aim for an original grand-strategy feel, not a CK3 clone. This is a standing implementation rule, not authorization for a full UI redesign.
21. **Admin / testing.** Kohl's Admin is command UI only, not gameplay authority. Game-state testing goes through `AdminBridge` into existing services. Do not scatter Kohl requires through gameplay services. Do not add client remotes for admin mutations. When implementing a new authoritative system, consider Admin Bridge hooks after its own APIs exist; do not invent deferred gameplay as fake admin commands. Ordinary Kohl rank is not GameTester. Global admin inspection may remain even when player UI is filtered. Convenient in-game copy/reuse of reusable IDs is a later pre-playtest admin UX requirement, not current work.
22. **Player-relevant information.** Ordinary player-facing UI must not automatically display global server state. Filter to the player, their ruler, their greater realm, things they interact with, or information designed as public. Admin/debug is exempt. `PlayerRelevantView` is a view helper, not gameplay authority.
23. **Terminology.** Territory = land/province. Title = political rank (Lord/Duke/King/Emperor). Do not interchange them.
24. **Pre-playtest usability.** Player/map orientation, coherent layout, and admin copy/reuse UX are required before the first published playtest. Do not implement them unless the Director authorizes it. Do not treat them as optional decoration on the roadmap.
25. **Economy / Resources V0.1** is **IMPLEMENTED — DIRECTOR-VERIFIED — DIRECTOR-ACCEPTED** (V0.1 Gold foundation only). Do not treat this as a complete economy. Do not implement buildings, development, taxation, trade, or delegated greater-realm permissions unless the Director authorizes it. Preserve Gold / Prestige / Influence as distinct match resources. Do not introduce Credits as a Gold replacement. Do not retrofit delegation into accepted V0.1 systems. Future Gold spends must go through `EconomyService`, not direct balance mutation. Kohl GameTester target tokens use `adminOwnerToken` / `adminRulerToken` (raw parse; suggestions are not authority). A landless liege with surviving subjects is an unresolved hierarchy/title case; do not modify accepted Hierarchy, Territory, Title, Diplomacy, or Warfare to “fix” it unless the Director designs it.
